      "question_id": "comparative_kant_hume_causation",
      "category": "comparative",
      "question": "Compare Hume's and Kant's accounts of causation, and explain why Kant\ntook Hume to have shown a problem that demanded a transcendental\nsolution.\n",
      "retrieved": [
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-on",
          "title": "raptor-subset-on",
          "url": null,
          "score": 0.8000823,
          "snippet": "Kant advances the claim that he has provided a complete solution to Hume's problem regarding causality by distinguishing between strict and comparative universality. He supports this assertion through his analysis in the Second Analogy, where he links these distinctions to Hume’s discussion of necessary connection while clarifying that their relationship is not deductive."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-on",
          "title": "raptor-subset-on",
          "url": null,
          "score": 0.7467331,
          "snippet": "Immanuel Kant argues that causality and other fundamental scientific principles are a priori categories of the understanding rather than empirical generalizations derived from Humean custom. By positing these transcendental concepts, he claims to resolve Hume's skepticism by grounding objective necessity and strict universality in the structure of human cognition instead of mere induction or subjective association."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-on",
          "title": "raptor-subset-on",
          "url": null,
          "score": 0.733627,
          "snippet": "Kant argues that while Hume correctly identifies the lack of empirical basis for causal necessity, he erroneously reduces it to subjective custom; Kant contends instead that causality and necessary connection are a priori categories contributed by the mind. This transcendental framework allows pure concepts to convert subjective perceptions into objective experience with strict universality, thereby grounding scientific knowledge in principles rather than mere induction."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-on",
          "title": "raptor-subset-on",
          "url": null,
          "score": 0.69096714,
          "snippet": "David Hume's skepticism regarding causality disrupted Immanuel Kant’s dogmatic slumber, prompting him to develop a revolutionary theory of experience grounded in a priori concepts. Although Kant initially explored solutions influenced by Hume in his pre-critical works, he ultimately rejected the idea that causal relations are derived from experience. Instead, Kant formulated synthetic a priori judgments as the foundation for metaphysics, positioning Hume as the philosopher who most closely approached but failed to solve this general problem."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-on",
          "title": "raptor-subset-on",
          "url": null,
          "score": 0.6729788,
          "snippet": "Kant argues that the concept of causality involves a necessary connection and strict universality, which cannot be derived empirically from repeated observations or constant conjunctions as Hume suggested. Instead, this a priori principle transforms subjective temporal sequences into objective events by positing that every effect follows necessarily from its cause according to universal rules."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-on",
          "title": "raptor-subset-on",
          "url": null,
          "score": 0.6691855,
          "snippet": "David Hume argues that causal knowledge is derived entirely from experience and custom rather than a priori reason, asserting that the connection between cause and effect cannot be logically deduced. Immanuel Kant responds by positing synthetic a priori judgments, claiming that categories of understanding such as causality are necessary conditions for experience itself, thereby providing objective validity to empirical observations."
        },
        {
          "corpus_id": "raptor-subset-on",
          "title": "raptor-subset-on",
          "url": null,
          "score": 0.64194757,
          "snippet": "Immanuel Kant developed his theory of synthetic a priori judgments to resolve the skepticism regarding causality and inductive reasoning raised by David Hume. While Hume viewed Newtonian laws as mere generalizations derived from experience, Kant argued they are foundational truths independent of empirical observation. This philosophical divergence highlights their contrasting approaches to metaphysics and scientific methodology."
        },
